WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Imagine your brain is a computer, right?

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.560
And, uh, someone has secretly opened like 50 browser tabs in the background.

3
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.000
Oh, that sounds like a nightmare.

4
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:09.480
Right.

5
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:10.560
You can't even see them.

6
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.960
You aren't actively clicking on them, but you can hear this faint

7
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.560
endless hum of static just coming through the speakers.

8
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.440
And meanwhile, because they're all running simultaneously, your

9
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.600
battery is draining incredibly fast.

10
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:23.960
Yeah.

11
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.880
And you have no idea why you're so exhausted.

12
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.680
Exactly.

13
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.400
You are completely drained and you don't even fully know why.

14
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.400
So today we're doing a deep dive into the invisible, um, well, the

15
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.440
engine of fear that drives our most common anxieties and more importantly,

16
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.080
how you can systematically shut those background tabs down.

17
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.440
I love that analogy.

18
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.720
And we're pulling from a really fascinating source today for this.

19
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.320
It's an audio segment from a medical test prep guide called Pass Ready Prep.

20
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:50.520
Yeah.

21
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.200
Hosted by an instructor named Keek, right?

22
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:52.600
Right.

23
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.160
And it's originally designed to help medical professionals differentiate

24
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.720
between psychological conditions for their board exams.

25
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.200
But, you know, we're taking this out of the clinic and

26
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.720
straight into your everyday life.

27
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:03.480
For sure.

28
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.440
Because Keek uses this brilliant framing device that completely

29
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.160
changes how we understand anxiety.

30
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:10.760
Yeah.

31
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.400
The core concept here is treating the human emotion of

32
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.280
fear like a mechanical engine.

33
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.240
Biologically, I mean, that engine is essentially our amygdala, that

34
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.440
ancient threat detection center in the brain.

35
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.120
And what Keek points out is that three of the most high prevalence

36
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.000
anxiety disorders out there, they all share this exact

37
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.680
same underlying engine.

38
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.240
They run on the exact same fuel.

39
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.000
Yes.

40
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.920
But they look completely different on the outside based on how that

